{"sections":[],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph"]]},"variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"cycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> ","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","text":"FunctionalGraph","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"typeIdentifier"}],"languages":["swift"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"content":[{"type":"heading","level":2,"text":"Discussion","anchor":"discussion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A counting sort of f gives the preimage rows and the in-degrees. Peeling the vertices of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"in-degree zero, repeatedly, visits the tree vertices leaves first, summing subtree sizes on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"the way, and leaves exactly the cyclic ones; each cycle is then walked from its least","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"vertex. Revisiting the peeled vertices in reverse, parents first, gives every tail length","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"and root and a preorder numbering of the trees from the subtree sizes; a counting sort by","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"depth then lays out the level-ancestor table. Nothing recurses or chases a chain of","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"dependent reads, so a tail of 10⁷ vertices needs no call stack.","type":"text"}]},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The graph has fewer than 2³¹ vertices.","type":"text"}]}],"type":"aside","style":"note"}],"kind":"content"}],"metadata":{"role":"symbol","externalID":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18cycleDecompositionAC05CycleE0VyF","extendedModule":"FunctionalGraphs","modules":[{"name":"FunctionalGraphs"}],"title":"cycleDecomposition()","symbolKind":"method","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","text":"FunctionalGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","text":"CycleDecomposition"}],"roleHeading":"Instance Method","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/FunctionalGraphs\/CycleDecomposition.swift#L269","fileName":"CycleDecomposition.swift"}},"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":"s (two "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" arrays for the preimage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex)."}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()"},"kind":"symbol","references":{"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/CycleDecomposition":{"title":"FunctionalGraph.CycleDecomposition","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/CycleDecomposition","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every cycle of a functional graph, and for each vertex its tail and the cycle it reaches, in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Flajolet and Odlyzko’s terms: walking from "},{"code":"v","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", the first vertex on a cycle is "},{"code":"root(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":","},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":"tailLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" steps away (Sage’s “preperiod”), on a cycle of "},{"code":"cycleLength(of: v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"vertices. For a permutation, "},{"code":"cycles","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is its cycle decomposition and every tail is zero."}],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition","type":"topic","role":"symbol","kind":"symbol","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"CycleDecomposition","kind":"identifier"}]},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs":{"title":"FunctionalGraphs","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs","type":"topic","role":"collection","kind":"symbol"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"FunctionalGraph"}],"navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"FunctionalGraph"}],"kind":"symbol","type":"topic","abstract":[{"text":"The graph of a function f on the vertices ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"0..<n"},{"text":": every vertex ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"v"},{"text":" has exactly one out-edge,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"v → f(v)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", and a fixed point is a self-loop. Also called a successor graph (Laaksonen,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Competitive Programmer’s Handbook"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":", §16.3).","type":"text"}],"title":"FunctionalGraph"},"doc://FunctionalGraphs/documentation/FunctionalGraphs/FunctionalGraph/cycleDecomposition()":{"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The cycles of the graph, and every vertex’s tail, root and preimages. O(n) time, with no"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"allocation per vertex. The result holds 5n + 3 "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":"s (two "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Int"},{"type":"text","text":" arrays for the preimage"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"rows, one for the vertices by depth, a 12-byte record per vertex and a 4-byte preorder"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number beside each vertex by depth), plus one per cycle and one per unit of the longest"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tail; building it takes 3n more for a moment (two 12-byte entries per vertex)."}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"cycleDecomposition"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV","text":"FunctionalGraph"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:16FunctionalGraphs0A5GraphV18CycleDecompositionV","text":"CycleDecomposition"}],"title":"cycleDecomposition()","kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/FunctionalGraphs\/documentation\/FunctionalGraphs\/FunctionalGraph\/cycleDecomposition()","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/functionalgraph\/cycledecomposition()"}}}